<header><p class="enc-kicker">Historical factor</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vassal and tributary systems</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">Vassal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tributary-system">tributary systems</a> describe the recurring pattern by which empires ruled indirectly through subordinate rulers who kept local authority in exchange for tribute, military service and acknowledgement of overlordship. Such systems allowed rapid expansion at low cost but were fragile, since vassals often broke away when central power weakened. The Hittite, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni</a> and Aztec empires and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit Empire</a> all relied heavily on this model.</p></div>
